     "text": "TAMPA — City officials announced Tuesday that a new water taxi service could start running between downtown Tampa and St. Petersburg as early as next summer.\n\nAccording to the plan, boats would leave the Tampa Convention Center every hour from 7 a.m. to 9 p.m. The trip across the bay would take about 45 minutes, which is reportedly faster than driving during rush hour. A one-way ticket is expected to cost between $10 and $15.\n\n“Our residents are tired of sitting in traffic,” the mayor said at a press conference. “This gives people a real choice for their commute.”\n\nNot everyone is convinced. Some residents worry about the cost to taxpayers, and environmental groups want to make sure the boats will not harm manatees in the bay. Officials say the boats will follow strict speed limits in manatee zones.\n\nThe project still needs to be approved by the city council, which will vote on it next month. If it is approved, a six-month trial will begin in the spring. Riders will be able to buy tickets through an app or at the dock.\n\nA similar service was tested in 2016 but was shut down after a few months because not enough people used it. This time, officials say, things will be different: the new boats are bigger, faster and air-conditioned.\n\nThe public can share their opinions at a meeting on November 12 at City Hall, or online on the city’s website.",
