     "type": "read",
     "title": "Blog: “My first Florida summer, explained”",
     "img": "l7_beach_flag",
     "text": "When I moved to Tampa from Chicago last spring, everyone warned me about the heat. “You’ll get used to it,” they said. I did not get used to it. Not in June, not in July, and definitely not in August.\n\nHere is what nobody explained. First, the heat is not the problem. The humidity is. At 8 a.m., you open the door and your glasses fog up. By lunchtime, the heat index is 105 and your shirt is stuck to your back. Locals call it “muggy.” I call it “living inside a mouth.”\n\nSecond, it rains every single afternoon, but only for about forty minutes. The sky turns black around 3 p.m., there’s a downpour so heavy you can’t see the car in front of you, and then the sun comes back like nothing happened. Plan your errands for the morning.\n\nThird, lightning is no joke. My apartment pool closes the second anyone hears thunder, and the lifeguards at the beach clear the water fast. At first I thought they were overreacting. Then I saw lightning hit a palm tree two blocks from my building.\n\nFourth, the wildlife is incredible. I’ve seen dolphins from the Skyway fishing pier, manatees in Apollo Beach, and an alligator sunbathing next to a retention pond behind the Publix. (I did not get close. Thirty feet, minimum.)\n\nFinally, the reward: October. One morning, it’s suddenly 72 degrees with low humidity, and the whole city seems to be smiling. People go jogging on Bayshore, restaurants open their patios, and someone in a puffer jacket says, “It’s chilly!” It isn’t. But I understand them now.",
