     "type": "read",
     "title": "Advice column: “Ask Annie”",
     "img": "l7_party",
     "text": "Dear Annie,\n\nI started a new job three months ago, and I still feel like an outsider. Everyone else seems to hit it off so easily. At lunch, they joke around and use expressions I don’t always understand. Last week my boss told me, “The ball is in your court,” and I honestly didn’t know what to do next. I’m sick and tired of feeling like the new kid. Help!\n\n— Lost in Lutz\n\nDear Lost,\n\nFirst of all, hang in there. Three months feels like forever, but most people need six months to a year to really learn the ropes at a new job. You’re not behind; you’re right on time.\n\nSecond, breaking the ice doesn’t have to be dramatic. Ask a coworker a simple question: “Where’s your go-to lunch spot?” or “Any plans for the weekend?” People love talking about themselves, and small conversations add up.\n\nThird, when someone uses an expression you don’t understand, ask! Saying “Wait, what does that mean?” with a smile is not embarrassing. It shows you’re curious, and it often starts a fun conversation. For the record, “the ball is in your court” means it’s your turn to make a decision or take action.\n\nFinally, go the extra mile, but don’t burn yourself out. Bring donuts one Friday. Offer to help someone who’s swamped. Little things like that make people remember you.\n\nBefore you know it, you’ll get the hang of the office culture, and someone new will be asking you what everything means.\n\n— Annie",
