     "text": "Last July, my neighbor Gloria called me at 6 a.m. She was whispering. “There’s an alligator in my pool,” she said. “A big one.”\n\nI had lived in Florida for only four months, and I had never seen a real alligator up close. I grabbed my phone, put on my flip-flops, and ran next door. By the time I got there, half the street had already gathered by her fence. Somebody had called the police, and somebody else was filming for Facebook.\n\nThere it was, floating in the deep end: a long, dark shape with bumpy skin and a huge head. It wasn’t moving. “It’s waiting,” whispered Mr. Patterson from across the street. “That’s how they hunt.”\n\nMeanwhile, Gloria’s cat was sitting on the edge of the pool, staring at the gator, completely calm. That should have been our first clue.\n\nTwenty minutes later, a trapper from the state wildlife office showed up with a long pole and a rope. He looked at the pool for about three seconds. Then he started laughing so hard he had to sit down.\n\nIt turned out the “alligator” was an inflatable pool toy. Gloria’s grandson had left it there after his birthday party the night before, and it had lost some of its air, so it looked surprisingly real in the early morning light.\n\nGloria was so embarrassed that she baked cookies for the whole street. The trapper took one for the road. And the video? It got two million views. Long story short: in Florida, always check twice before you call for help. But also, never stop checking.",
     "gloss": {
      "whispering": "speaking very quietly",
      "gathered": "came together in a group",
      "trapper": "a person whose job is to catch animals",
      "inflatable": "filled with air",
      "clue": "a small sign that helps you understand something"
     },
