     "img": "dollars",
     "text": "Moving to the United States means learning a whole new money system. For many newcomers, the biggest surprise is not the prices, but the importance of something they had never heard of before: the credit score.\n\nA credit score is a three-digit number, usually between 300 and 850, that tells lenders how likely you are to pay back money you borrow. Landlords often check it before renting you an apartment, and it can affect the interest rate on a car loan. The problem? When you arrive, you usually have no credit history at all, so it can be hard to get approved for anything.\n\nFinancial educators say newcomers often start with a secured credit card. You give the bank a deposit, for example $500, and that becomes your credit limit. If you use the card for small purchases and pay the full balance on time every month, you slowly build a credit history.\n\nThe second big change is taxes. In the US, income tax is usually withheld from each paycheck, and every spring you file a tax return. If too much was withheld, you receive a refund; if not enough, you owe the difference. In January, your employer will send you a W-2. Keep it in a safe place.\n\nThird, many Americans are paid every two weeks, which means some months have three paychecks. Budget experts often suggest building your monthly budget around two paychecks and treating the third one as a bonus for savings.\n\nFinally, take advantage of free resources. Many public libraries offer free tax help in the spring, and some banks have financial counselors. Every situation is different, so it’s smart to ask questions before making big decisions.",
